steps.body:
Put a `select` inside a `for` loop to receive from several channels as values arrive. Use the two-value receive to notice when an input closes.

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

func produce(nums []int) <-chan int {
	out := make(chan int)
	go func() {
		defer close(out)
		for _, n := range nums {
			out <- n
		}
	}()
	return out
}

func main() {
	a := produce([]int{1, 2, 3})
	b := produce([]int{10, 20, 30})
	sum := 0
	{{code}}
	fmt.Println("sum:", sum)
}
```

```go
for a != nil || b != nil {
	select {
	case v, ok := <-a:
		if !ok {
			a = nil
			continue
		}
		sum += v // use v
	case v, ok := <-b:
		if !ok { // same for b
			b = nil
			continue
		}
		sum += v
	}
}
```

Why `a = nil`? A closed channel is always ready, so its case would fire again and again with zero values. A send or receive on a **nil channel is never ready**, so setting it to nil switches that case off. The loop ends when every input is nil. `continue` inside the `select` continues the `for` loop, but a plain `break` only leaves the `select`, just like in a `switch`.

Which channel wins each round depends on scheduling, so the arrival order changes between runs. Combine the merged values in a way that does not care about order: a total, a count, or a slice you sort before printing.

example.code:
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sort"
)

func produce(nums []int) <-chan int {
	out := make(chan int)
	go func() {
		defer close(out)
		for _, n := range nums {
			out <- n
		}
	}()
	return out
}

func main() {
	a := produce([]int{1, 2, 3})
	b := produce([]int{10, 20, 30})
	var got []int
	sum := 0
	for a != nil || b != nil {
		select {
		case v, ok := <-a:
			if !ok {
				a = nil // a nil channel is never ready, so this case turns off
				continue
			}
			got = append(got, v)
			sum += v
		case v, ok := <-b:
			if !ok {
				b = nil
				continue
			}
			got = append(got, v)
			sum += v
		}
	}
	sort.Ints(got) // arrival order depends on scheduling
	fmt.Println(got, sum)
}

