steps.body:
A generator that loops forever needs a way to hear "stop". The usual signal is a `done chan struct{}`. Its values take zero bytes, and nobody ever sends on it. The side that decides to stop, here `main`, just closes it.

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

func naturals(done <-chan struct{}) <-chan int {
	out := make(chan int)
	go func() {
		defer close(out)
		for n := 1; ; n++ {
			{{code}}
		}
	}()
	return out
}

func main() {
	done := make(chan struct{})
	nums := naturals(done)
	fmt.Println(<-nums, <-nums, <-nums)
	close(done)
	for range nums {
	}
	fmt.Println("generator stopped")
}
```

```go
select {
case out <- n:
case <-done:
	return
}
```

A receive from a closed channel is ready right away, so `close(done)` wakes **every** goroutine waiting on `<-done` at once. One close can stop many workers, which a single send never could. Afterwards, drain the output with `for range out { }`: a range with no variables receives and discards each value, and ends when the generator closes its channel.

The parameter type `<-chan struct{}` is receive-only, so the generator can listen but cannot close `done` by mistake.

**Gotcha:** without `done`, a generator blocks forever on `out <- n` as soon as main stops receiving. That is a goroutine leak. The garbage collector never frees a blocked goroutine, so its stack and everything it references stay in memory until the program exits.

example.code:
package main

import "fmt"

func naturals(done <-chan struct{}) <-chan int {
	out := make(chan int)
	go func() {
		defer close(out)
		for n := 1; ; n++ {
			select {
			case out <- n:
			case <-done:
				return // done was closed, so stop instead of blocking forever
			}
		}
	}()
	return out
}

func main() {
	done := make(chan struct{})
	nums := naturals(done)
	var got []int
	for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
		got = append(got, <-nums)
	}
	fmt.Println(got)

	close(done)
	for range nums {
		// discard values until the generator sees done and closes nums
	}
	fmt.Println("generator stopped")
}

